      "value": "I'm not an expert in computer rendering, but from what I know:\nThe main advantage of Deferred Rendering is that, instead of rendering each object and the respective light being cast, it renders all objects on-screen into a depth buffer (a collection of distance data away from your point of view) and combines all of the lighting render passes no that data. This way you wouldn't have to render objects and lighting being occluded. \nThe main problem is that Deferred Rendering doesn't support MSAA (MSAA works by basically rendering only the object data at a higher resolution, so you get sharp edges, but textures within objects stay the same). This is why most VR games are rendered using Forward Rendering, including really optimized ones like Half-life: Alyx.\nTAA (used by flatscreen games) is the solution to not being able to use MSAA, by using temporal information of the camera shaking very slightly and kind of combining the data. The problem with using this in VR is that we are way too good at noticing if something is not being persistent within our vision, therefore we notice when there are artifacts caused by combining frames at different points in time. It causes smearing.\nScreen-Space Reflections (probably the cool reflections you were talking about) also work with Forward Rendering, you can try them here, for example: https://vrchat.com/home/launch?worldId=wrld_c70b4944-7474-44c8-ac5b-ca9ad805b6dd\nI think the biggest problem with performance in VRChat is not resolution, object or lighting related (you can drop down the resolution in a crowded world and still have almost the same bad performance). It is with unoptimized player-made content (you can't expect normal players to have the same level of patience a developer has).\nI think a system most benefitting VRChat would be something like Unreal's Nanite, an automated way to decimate avatars (maybe based on your performance setting?)",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
